1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:06,720
Module 4, Video 4.5: Reviewing the model, what it assumed, and what it could not express.

2
00:00:07,920 --> 00:00:08,080
Um,

3
00:00:09,140 --> 00:00:14,560
imagine an AI assistant generating the core configuration file for your ministry's newest application.

4
00:00:14,900 --> 00:00:16,700
Oh, yeah. That's a very common scenario these days.

5
00:00:16,940 --> 00:00:17,060
Right.

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:21,720
But what happens when that AI encounters a scenario nobody wrote down?

7
00:00:22,540 --> 00:00:25,580
Or, I don't know, a task the platform simply cannot do.

8
00:00:26,180 --> 00:00:29,940
Does it alert you, or does it just quietly decide on its own?

9
00:00:30,820 --> 00:00:33,540
Well, when systems quietly decide things, um,

10
00:00:34,400 --> 00:00:36,240
you basically lose control of your own policy.

11
00:00:36,440 --> 00:00:36,980
Exactly.

12
00:00:37,940 --> 00:00:47,260
So today's deep dive explores how to maintain absolute control over digital models in your programs, ensuring system limitations and missing information are explicitly managed,

13
00:00:47,780 --> 00:00:49,140
uh, rather than silently guessed.

14
00:00:49,780 --> 00:00:51,660
Right. So let's start at the beginning.

15
00:00:52,680 --> 00:00:56,240
How does agreed information actually reach the applications file?

16
00:00:57,020 --> 00:01:00,400
So everything agreed upon reaches the file in one of four specific ways.

17
00:01:00,520 --> 00:01:02,100
Okay. First, it's placed directly.

18
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:06,900
Second, it's placed by a judgment, uh, which is written down as an assumption.

19
00:01:07,140 --> 00:01:08,820
Written down as an assumption. Exactly. Got it.

20
00:01:09,420 --> 00:01:14,920
Third, it cannot be placed, so it is written down as a loss, and fourth, it just, you know, stays in its own document.

21
00:01:15,340 --> 00:01:20,200
Okay, so thinking about this, it's almost like packing a really complex cargo flight.

22
00:01:20,420 --> 00:01:21,360
Oh, I like that analogy.

23
00:01:21,580 --> 00:01:27,680
Yeah. So standard boxes load directly. Oddly shaped items, well, they require a judgment call, so those are your assumptions.

24
00:01:28,360 --> 00:01:30,660
Oversized items are left behind. Those are the losses.

25
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:33,060
And some items just, you know, stay in the warehouse.

26
00:01:33,540 --> 00:01:42,180
That is a perfect way to picture it, and we really need to clarify the difference between those judgment calls and the left behind items because, well, that's where faults originate.

27
00:01:42,680 --> 00:01:47,840
Right. So if an assumption is like a decision made because the original documents were silent,

28
00:01:48,520 --> 00:01:50,400
what exactly defines a loss?

29
00:01:50,980 --> 00:01:54,240
A loss is an agreed limitation that the file cannot express.

30
00:01:54,740 --> 00:01:57,020
It sits alongside a note of what is being done instead.

31
00:01:57,460 --> 00:02:00,660
Both of these assumptions and losses are normal parts of the process.

32
00:02:01,180 --> 00:02:07,040
But wait, I mean, if the goal is efficiency here, why shouldn't the AI simply fill in these gaps automatically?

33
00:02:07,240 --> 00:02:10,039
Isn't the whole appeal of an AI assistant to save time?

34
00:02:10,440 --> 00:02:16,900
I hear that argument a lot, but a gap filled silently by invention actually becomes a fault in the running service.

35
00:02:17,180 --> 00:02:18,760
Oh, wow. Wait, really?

36
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:28,900
Yes. If nobody is told, it leads directly to systems that are fragmented, siloed, duplicated, rekeyed, locked into one supplier, and

37
00:02:29,020 --> 00:02:30,000
costly to change.

38
00:02:30,560 --> 00:02:33,220
That sounds incredibly risky for any ministry.

39
00:02:33,460 --> 00:02:42,220
It is. The AI must refuse to guess because, you know, a gap it names can be ruled on, but a gap it fills silently is just a liability.

40
00:02:43,060 --> 00:02:49,360
So how do you review a system to catch those gaps without, you know, forcing a director general to read the raw code itself?

41
00:02:49,920 --> 00:02:51,400
Well, you don't read the application file.

42
00:02:51,620 --> 00:02:56,160
Instead, you read plain language cards. You get one for each assumption and one for each loss.

43
00:02:56,500 --> 00:02:57,500
Okay. Cards?

44
00:02:58,220 --> 00:03:00,220
Can we ground this with a specific example?

45
00:03:00,540 --> 00:03:00,740
Sure.

46
00:03:01,400 --> 00:03:03,780
Let's look at the Ministry of Education in Progressa.

47
00:03:04,200 --> 00:03:06,840
Progressa, which is the fictional country of these examples.

48
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:09,560
Right, Progressa. Imagine two cards are generated.

49
00:03:09,980 --> 00:03:17,780
First, the assumption that a change of name takes effect on the day the minister approves it, uh, because the original policy story document didn't specify.

50
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:25,000
Makes sense. Then the loss that the platform cannot send an institutional notice, so it's kept as a manual task for an officer.

51
00:03:25,660 --> 00:03:27,780
Okay, so what happens with these two cards?

52
00:03:28,120 --> 00:03:34,920
The director of higher education reads these plain language cards, rules yes on both, and records her name and the date.

53
00:03:35,760 --> 00:03:39,000
But what if the director says no to that name change assumption?

54
00:03:39,620 --> 00:03:42,400
Like, what if it actually needs to wait for official publication?

55
00:03:42,860 --> 00:03:47,460
That is a great question. The rejection is never simply typed into the file to patch it.

56
00:03:48,340 --> 00:03:52,120
Because patching directly means the code and the policy document fall out of sync.

57
00:03:52,540 --> 00:03:56,840
Exactly. It triggers a strict loop to maintain a single source of truth.

58
00:03:56,940 --> 00:03:59,720
So first, the story is corrected. Right.

59
00:03:59,940 --> 00:04:03,560
Second, the file is written again. Third, the cards are read again.

60
00:04:03,860 --> 00:04:07,860
Uh-huh. And fourth, approval waits until every card carries a ruling.

61
00:04:08,480 --> 00:04:11,440
Wow. So it is a very rigorous process. So

62
00:04:12,100 --> 00:04:19,300
to summarize, before approving new systems for your program, you must demand every assumption and laws as a plain language card.

63
00:04:19,680 --> 00:04:20,180
Absolutely.

64
00:04:20,899 --> 00:04:23,980
You rule on each one and record who ruled and when.

